\CKN@Section{Theoretical Foundations}

Early in this treatise, we'll explain the theoretical foundations that relate to, and pertain to compiler construction. This includes a good amount of math, some linguistics, systems programming, data structures and algorithms, and other disciplines related to theory of programming language implementation.

Before delving further, it is admittedly true that there's not a single ``uniform'' science that is called \emph{Programming Language Theory}, let alone, \emph{Theory of Compiler Construction}! The issue is, academics need to eat. That is a given. So when they submit for funds, they don't really want the board at their university to ask them a simple question: ``What is the point?''. And truly, what is the point?

I realize that, there's universities all around the globe where a theory that is called ``Programming Language Theory'' is thought. In \CKN@Ref{appendix:A} you'll see a large list of scientists, some alive and well, some alive not-so-well, and some buried sxi feet under, who have tried and compiled\footnote{No pun intended} tome after tome on what the literature often calls \emph{concepts of programming languages}. But let's not carried away.

Truth is, programming languages are a culmination of five thousand years of people trying to find ways to solve problems in real world without having to waste too much time. If you are a Phonecian merchant, you don't want to wait until the next child sacrifice sesh\footnote{It is contested whether they burned children alive, or only the ones who died at birth} to hedge your bets. You need your underlings to compute, calculate, and tabulate FAST. It is the urge to compute, calculate and tabulate that we have gone from using our fingers, to clay tablets, to base-ten digits, to logarithms, to adding machines, and we finally arrived at the Von Neumann machine. I doubt we're stuck here. Even if tomorrow, we slid into another dark ages, and all the world forgot how to build computers; having scavengers kill each other Mad Max-style for a stick of DRAM, we still have a long time to go before the earth is total. People are lazy, and they wear their laziness on their sleeves. We'll make progress, if not today, a millenia from now.

\par\noindent
\CKN@Box[aside]{%
  You have to realize, humanity's still at its precipice. 
}

